      "problem": "Factor the expression $y^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} x + a^2 =  (x+{\\color{blue}{a}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\ny^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with ${\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
